{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The best of his field and the best of his vineyard he shall pay.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, proved from here that damages are appraised from the best quality land — for if you were to say from middling or from inferior quality, each person would rob his fellow's best land and pay from the inferior or from the middling. Therefore the Torah says: 'the best of his field and the best of his vineyard he shall pay' — that he is obligated to pay the injured party from his best quality, so that he will say: 'Why should I rob and extort? Tomorrow the court will descend upon my property and take my fine field, relying on what is written in the Torah: the best of his field and the best of his vineyard he shall pay.' And this is what we learned: damages are appraised from the best quality (be-idit), a creditor from middling quality (be-binonit), and a woman's marriage-settlement from inferior quality (be-ziburit). The rule that a creditor collects from inferior quality is from the Torah, as it is said (Deuteronomy 24:11): 'בחוץ תעמוד והאיש אשר אתה נושה בו יוציא אליך את העבוט החוצה' — and the way of a householder is to take out the least of his utensils. But the Sages enacted that he may collect from middling quality, for if you say from inferior quality, you lock the door before borrowers. A woman's marriage-settlement is from inferior quality — the reason being that a man desires to marry a woman more than a woman desires to be married."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:51.534801829Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}